---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FigureCard from "../components/FigureCard.astro";
import StatBlock from "../components/StatBlock.astro";
import { findings } from "../data/findings";

const headlineStats = [
  { label: "matches", value: "104" },
  { label: "markets", value: "312" },
  { label: "P", value: "2.6×10⁻⁷" },
];

const outline = [
  { href: "#question", label: "Questions" },
  { href: "#data", label: "Data" },
  { href: "#method", label: "Methods" },
  { href: "#result", label: "Results" },
  { href: "#takeaway", label: "Takeaways" },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Home">
  <section>
    <h1>World Cup 2026 Prediction Markets</h1>
    <p>
     An analysis of Kalshi's 2026 World Cup prediction markets.
    </p>
    <nav class="page-outline" aria-label="Page sections">
      {
        outline.map((o, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && <span aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>}
            <a href={o.href}>{o.label}</a>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </nav>
  </section>

  <br>

  <section id="question">
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Do betting markets react more to the final result of a match or to how well a team actually played?</li>
      <li>Does the market react to new information rationally, or does it show behavioral biases such as <strong>overreacting</strong> (a move that partially reverses) or <strong>underreacting</strong> (a move that keeps drifting, as if catching up)?</li>
      <li>Is efficiency uniform across matches, or does it vary with how much <strong>public attention</strong> a match draws?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>Data</h2>
    <p>
      Includes all 104 matches of the 2026 World Cup, with three sources joined by team identity and kickoff date.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Source</th><th>Coverage</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Kalshi &mdash; KXWCGAME</td>
          <td>3-way (home/away/tie) regulation-time moneyline contract per match; minute-resolution price, volume, and open-interest candles.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Kalshi &mdash; KXMENWORLDCUP</td>
          <td>One tournament-winner futures contract per team; hourly candles spanning the full tournament.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>FBref (Opta-sourced)</td>
          <td>Official match and player statistics: shooting, cards, fouls, lineups, event timelines.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SofaScore</td>
          <td>Expected goals (xG) and ~45 other team statistics split by half; minute-stamped goals/cards/subs; per-minute momentum index.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="method">
    <h2>Methods</h2>
    <p>There were four main stages of my analysis, each meant to examine a different piece of the questions above:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Regression</strong>: I regressed price movement on goal margin and xG margin to see which one it tracks more closely.</li>
      <li><strong>Event studies</strong>: I split price reactions into an immediate <em>jump</em> and a subsequent <em>drift</em>; a drift that opposes the jump is a sign of overreaction, and a drift that continues with the jump indicates underreaction.</li>
      <li><strong>Calibration</strong>: To evaluate whether a price of X% actually happens X% of the time, I sampled prices at eight fixed checkpoints preceding each contract’s own settlement (3 h to 5 min) and paired it with the eventual binary outcome. I then assessed calibration with two standard measures: Brier score (mean squared error between price and outcome, where lower values indicate better calibration) and a reliability diagram (binned price against realized frequency, with 95% Wilson confidence intervals).</li>
      <li><strong>Heterogeneity</strong>: Because the calibration design produced only one pooled statistic, I also modeled each observation’s squared error directly. I regressed squared error on standardized log trading volume and open interest, with checkpoint fixed effects to account for changes as settlement approaches.</li>
    </ul>
    <!-- 
    <p>
      Note: team names are not internally consistent even within Kalshi's own series (e.g. Turkiye vs. Turkey), and SofaScore differs from both on a further eight teams (e.g. Cape Verde vs. Cabo Verde); I joined the datasets with a manual crosswalk and validated against a zero-unmatched-observations check. 
    </p>
    -->
    
  </section>

  <section id="result">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Goal margin dominates price movement; xG margin barely registers once both are in the same model. Across four independent tests, the market shows no reliable overreaction or underreaction. But pooled efficiency hides real structure: the most heavily traded matches are priced measurably <em>less</em> accurately than quiet ones &mdash; a pattern that survives, and even strengthens, once tournament stage is controlled for.
    </p>
    <StatBlock stats={headlineStats} />
    <p>on the attention-vs-calibration effect &mdash; the full set of tests, in the order the argument builds:</p>

    <div id="findings-list">
      {
        findings.map((f) => (
          <section id={f.slug}>
            <h3>{f.title}</h3>
            <p>{f.summary}</p>
            <StatBlock stats={f.stats} />
            {f.figures.map((fig) => (
              <FigureCard src={fig.src} alt={fig.alt} caption={fig.caption} />
            ))}
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaway">
    <h2>Takeaways</h2>
    <div class="closing-box">
      <p>
        This market gets the fundamentals right &mdash; it tracks results, and it doesn't show a directional behavioral bias &mdash; but its accuracy isn't uniform. Attention and pricing accuracy pull in opposite directions, which matters most exactly where a market is trusted most: the matches everyone is watching.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore">Explore the data &rarr;</a><br />
        <a href="https://github.com/kate-marine/wc26-prediction-markets" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub repository &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
